{
  "id": "c13",
  "title": "Addresses and Pointers",
  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "c13-l1",
      "title": "& and *",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "Every variable lives somewhere in memory, at an address. &x gives the address of x. A pointer is a variable that holds such an address: int *p = &x; means \"p points to x\".",
        "*p follows the pointer: it is the variable p points to. Reading *p reads x, and *p = 7; changes x.",
        "Pointers are the heart of C. They're why C was fast enough to write a whole operating system in, and why its bugs can be so nasty."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "#include <stdio.h>",
          "",
          "int main() {",
          "    int x = 5;",
          "    int *p = &x;",
          "    printf(\"x = %d\\n\", x);",
          "    printf(\"*p = %d\\n\", *p);",
          "    *p = 7;",
          "    printf(\"x = %d\\n\", x);",
          "    return 0;",
          "}"
        ],
        "output": [
          "x = 5",
          "*p = 5",
          "x = 7"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "c13-l1-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does &x give?",
          "options": [
            "The address of x",
            "The value of x",
            "x times 2",
            "A copy of x"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "& is the address-of operator."
        },
        {
          "id": "c13-l1-e2",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does int *p declare?",
          "options": [
            "A pointer to an int",
            "An int called *p",
            "A multiplication",
            "An array"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "The * in a declaration means \"pointer to\"."
        },
        {
