    {
      "id": "c13-l2",
      "title": "Changing Variables from Functions",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "Remember: functions get copies. But if you pass the address of a variable, the function can follow it and change the original. That's exactly what scanf(\"%d\", &n) does.",
        "void triple(int *p) takes a pointer. Inside, *p = *p * 3; changes the caller's variable. Call it with triple(&x);.",
        "This is how a C function gives back more than one result: through pointer parameters."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "#include <stdio.h>",
          "",
          "void triple(int *p) {",
          "    *p = *p * 3;",
          "}",
          "",
          "int main() {",
          "    int x = 4;",
          "    triple(&x);",
          "    printf(\"%d\\n\", x);",
          "    return 0;",
          "}"
        ],
        "output": [
          "12"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "c13-l2-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "Why does triple(&x) change x, but triple(x) couldn't?",
          "options": [
            "It gets x's address and changes x through it",
            "&x makes x global",
            "It's faster",
            "It doesn't change x"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "The function follows the address back to the real x."
        },
        {
          "id": "c13-l2-e2",
